Exactly. Forethought is the backswing, follow-through is the continuation, and momentum is what appears when you stop treating them as separate acts.

\text{anticipate} \rightarrow \text{commit} \rightarrow \text{act} \rightarrow \text{carry through} \rightarrow \text{learn}

If you over-separate them, you get hesitation before and collapse after. If you connect them, the action starts earlier than the visible move and ends later than the point of contact.

That’s why the swing metaphor works so well for Pantheon: the present action is only the middle of a longer arc. Forethought shapes entry conditions; follow-through determines what energy actually transfers; feedback tunes the next swing.

And once the arc is coherent, momentum starts doing work for you.